ows between  ports and trade lanes and while they  have their own information and can  clearly see what works for them in the  the viewpoint of their company what  they're missing what they've been  missing previously was the ability to  look at data from other shipping lines  other nvocc s other freight forwarders  and then make value add propositions to  customers based on their ability to peel  the customers off other people's  business and add them to their own trade  lanes for less money so what people will  do is they will maybe analyze a port  let's say I'm interested in the port of  LA which is what I've pulled up in this  particular instance and we can quickly  get a sense of where trade is coming  from clicking into each one of these  will actually expand more specific  statistics related to it but this is a  great overview now if I decide hey I  want to do business in the port of LA  but I only have maybe ten million  dollars to spend on I'm contracting on  port space on whatever provisions I need  to get to that port I can actually very  quickly in two steps go through all all  60 million 66 million u.s. import  records that we have on the system by  clicking two buttons see the top players  by tu by matching shipments by weight in  some cases by value in some cases by  taxation my certifications I can select  the individual things that I would need  to have like hazmat permits and things  of that nature and it will automatically  Korra just collate that data for you and  beneath this is more information about  each individual company so it's a really  interesting case that takes away from  having to kind of guess about your  competitors activities to actually  knowing what they're doing in your port  I think the most advanced case goes back  to the the third and the fourth delivery  methods that we briefly talked about  which our API and custom solutions and  that's in this particular instance  governments general Amos governments as  Peter said are very interested in  promoting positive trade and making sure  that bad things don't come across their  borders which can be very difficult and  one of the difficulties with the data  that Peter didn't touch on is that not  only do people have a pathological  aversion to spell  correctly or spell checking but people  actually specifically game the system  they will actually work in one country  to obfuscate what they're shipping to  prevent paying tariffs or to prevent  being inspected or to prevent having bad  food stopped at a port and they will do  that the entire time that the ship is in  transit to their benefit and it's a very  common occurrence so governments of  course are interested in understanding  how are we going to stop these shipments  we can't have a bunch of people at every  port looking at every container but what  we can do is compare container  descriptions across country borders and  understand what one government is  inspecting it might not care about and  what we do care about or vice versa so  in this case we're looking for these  anomalies at a very large scale and  there's no really beautiful slide for me  to show you about that because it's a  lot of just data work but that's kind of  our most advanced case so what's the  business take away that the fact is it's  actually the same as the technical take  away which is we need domain expertise  it's really interesting to just pull  stats from the data to run basic  analyses on the information to find kind  of anomalous readings using just math  which I like to do on my own but the the  problem is that each individual company  has a specific need and while we know we  can't answer each specific need we know  we can't answer the kind of super class  of question and that means we work very  very closely with our customers with the  government's we work with with our data  partners and then we connect them  directly to our engineering departments  so there's a lot of customer and  government to engi